#---
# purpose: "a clause that calls a closure with one argument beside a clause that calls it with three, each guarded by is_function/2, compiles and runs the clause whose arity the closure has"
# kind: run
#---
#
# `slicer` is an arity-3 closure carrying `k`. The first clause's call
# `slicer.(e)` names one argument, so it can never run that closure: it
# contributes no flow to the closure's construction, and the guard
# `is_function(slicer, 1)` is false at run time, so the second clause runs.
defmodule Main do
  def pick({:ok, _count, slicer}, e) when is_function(slicer, 1), do: slicer.(e)
  def pick({:ok, _count, slicer}, _e) when is_function(slicer, 3), do: slicer.(1, 2, 1)
  def make(k), do: {:ok, 3, fn a, b, c -> [k, a, b, c] end}
  def main(), do: IO.inspect(pick(make(7), 1))
end
Main.main()
